fm_doc::register_doc! {
    name: "clear",
    aliases: [],
    section: "inspection",
    summary: "Clear or Delete a Variable",
    body: r#"
## Usage
Clears a set of variables from the current context, or alternately,
delete all variables defined in the current context.  There are
several formats for the function call.  The first is the explicit form
in which a list of variables are provided:

```text
   clear a1 a2 ...
```
The variables can be persistent or global, and they will be deleted.
The second form

```text
   clear 'all'
```
clears all variables and libraries from the current context.  Alternately, you can
use the form:

```text
   clear 'libs'
```
which will unload any libraries or DLLs that have been `import`ed.
Optionally, you can specify that persistent variables should be cleared via:

```text
   clear 'persistent'
```
and similarly for global variables:

```text
   clear 'global'
```
You can use

```text
   clear 'classes'
```
to clear all definitions of user-defined classes.
With no arguments, `clear` defaults to clearing `'all'`.

## Example
Here is a simple example of using `clear` to delete a variable.  First, we create a variable called `a`:

```fm-exec
a = 53
```

Next, we clear `a` using the `clear` function, and verify that it is deleted.

```fm-exec
# errors: 1
clear a
a
```
"#,
}

fm_doc::register_doc! {
    name: "end",
    aliases: [],
    section: "inspection",
    summary: "End Function",
    body: r#"
## Usage
Computes the size of a variable along a given dimension.  The syntax
for its use is

```text
   y = end(x,dim,subindexes)
```
where `x` is the array to be analyzed, `dim` is the dimension along
which to compute the end, and `subindexes` indicates how many dimensions
are involved in the `end` calculation.
"#,
}

fm_doc::register_doc! {
    name: "exist",
    aliases: [],
    section: "inspection",
    summary: "Test for Existence",
    body: r#"
## Usage
Tests for the existence of a variable, function, directory or
file.  The general syntax for its use is

```text
  y = exist(item,kind)
```
where `item` is a string containing the name of the item
to look for, and `kind` is a string indicating the type
of the search.  The `kind` must be one of
- `'builtin'` checks for built-in functions

- `'dir'` checks for directories

- `'file'` checks for files

- `'var'` checks for variables

- `'all'` checks all possibilities (same as leaving out `kind`)

You can also leave the `kind` specification out, in which case
the calling syntax is

```text
  y = exist(item)
```
The return code is one of the following:
- 0 - if `item` does not exist

- 1 - if `item` is a variable in the workspace

- 2 - if `item` is an M file on the search path, a full pathname
 to a file, or an ordinary file on your search path

- 5 - if `item` is a built-in FreeMat function

- 7 - if `item` is a directory

Note: previous to version `1.10`, `exist` used a different notion
of existence for variables: a variable was said to exist if it
was defined and non-empty.  This test is now performed by `isset`.

## Example
Some examples of the `exist` function.  Note that generally `exist`
is used in functions to test for keywords.  For example,

```text
  function y = testfunc(a, b, c)
  if (~exist('c'))
    % c was not defined, so establish a default
    c = 13;
  end
  y = a + b + c;
```
An example of `exist` in action.

```fm-exec
a = randn(3,5,2)
b = []
who
exist('a')
exist('b')
exist('c')
```
"#,
}

fm_doc::register_doc! {
    name: "fieldnames",
    aliases: [],
    section: "inspection",
    summary: "Fieldnames of a Structure",
    body: r#"
## Usage
Returns a cell array containing the names of the fields in
a structure array.  The syntax for its use is

```text
   x = fieldnames(y)
```
where `y` is a structure array of object array.  The result
is a cell array, with one entry per field in `y`.

## Example
We define a simple structure array:

```fm-exec
y.foo = 3; y.goo = 'hello';
x = fieldnames(y)
```
"#,
}

fm_doc::register_doc! {
    name: "isa",
    aliases: [],
    section: "inspection",
    summary: "Test Type of Variable",
    body: r#"
## Usage
Tests the type of a variable.  The syntax for its
use is

```text
   y = isa(x,type)
```
where `x` is the variable to test, and `type` is
the type.  Supported built-in types are
- `'cell'` for cell-arrays

- `'struct'` for structure-arrays

- `'logical'` for logical arrays

- `'uint8'` for unsigned 8-bit integers

- `'int8'` for signed 8-bit integers

- `'uint16'` for unsigned 16-bit integers

- `'int16'` for signed 16-bit integers

- `'uint32'` for unsigned 32-bit integers

- `'int32'` for signed 32-bit integers

- `'uint64'` for unsigned 64-bit integers

- `'int64'` for signed 64-bit integers

- `'single'` for 32-bit floating point numbers

- `'double'` for 64-bit floating point numbers

- `'char'` for string arrays

If the argument is a user-defined type (via the `class` function), then
the name of that class is returned.

## Examples
Here are some examples of the `isa` call.

```fm-exec
a = {1}
isa(a,'char')
isa(a,'cell')
```

Here we use `isa` along with shortcut boolean evaluation to
safely determine if a variable contains the string `'hello'`

```fm-exec
a = 'hello'
isa(a,'char') && strcmp(a,'hello')
```
"#,
}

