fm_doc::register_doc! {
    name: "isinf",
    aliases: [],
    section: "inspection",
    summary: "Test for infinities",
    body: r#"
## Usage
Returns true for entries of an array that are infs (i.e.,
infinities).  The usage is

```text
   y = isinf(x)
```
The result is a logical array of the same size as `x`,
which is true if `x` is not-a-number, and false otherwise.
Note that for `complex` or `dcomplex` data types that
the result is true if either the real or imaginary parts
are infinite.

## Example
Suppose we have an array of floats with one element that
is `inf`:

```fm-exec
a = [1.2 3.4 inf 5]
isinf(a)
b = 3./[2 5 0 3 1]
```
"#,
}

fm_doc::register_doc! {
    name: "isinttype",
    aliases: [],
    section: "inspection",
    summary: "Test For Integer-type Array",
    body: r#"
## Usage
The syntax for `isinttype` is

```text
   x = isinttype(y)
```
and it returns a logical 1 if the argument is an integer type
and a logical 0 otherwise.  Note that this function only tests
the type of the variable, not the value.  So if, for example,
`y` is a `float` array containing all integer values, it will
still return a logical 0.
"#,
}

