fm_doc::register_doc! {
    name: "length",
    aliases: [],
    section: "inspection",
    summary: "Length of an Array",
    body: r#"
## Usage
Returns the length of an array `x`.  The syntax for its use
is

```text
   y = length(x)
```
and is defined as the maximum length of `x` along any of its
dimensions, i.e., `max(size(x))`.  If you want to determine the
number of elements in `x`, use the `numel` function instead.

## Example
For a `4 x 4 x 3` matrix, the length is `4`, not `48`, as you
might expect.

```fm-exec
x = rand(4,4,3);
length(x)
```
"#,
}

fm_doc::register_doc! {
    name: "maxdim",
    aliases: [],
    section: "inspection",
    summary: "Maximum Dimension in Array",
    body: r#"
## Usage
The `maxdim` function returns the lowest order dimension
along which an array is largest.  The general syntax for its
use is

```text
  n = maxdim(x)
```
and is equivalent to min(find(size(x) == max(size(x)))).
"#,
}

fm_doc::register_doc! {
    name: "ndims",
    aliases: [],
    section: "inspection",
    summary: "Number of Dimensions in Array",
    body: r#"
## Usage
The `ndims` function returns the number of dimensions
allocated in an array.  The general syntax for its
use is

```text
  n = ndims(x)
```
and is equivalent to `length(size(x))`.
"#,
}

fm_doc::register_doc! {
    name: "nnz",
    aliases: [],
    section: "inspection",
    summary: "Number of Nonzeros",
    body: r#"
## Usage
Returns the number of nonzero elements in a matrix.
The general format for its use is

```text
   y = nnz(x)
```
This function returns the number of nonzero elements
in a matrix or array.  This function works for both
sparse and non-sparse arrays.  For

## Example

```fm-exec
a = [1,0,0,5;0,3,2,0]
nnz(a)
A = sparse(a)
nnz(A)
```
"#,
}

fm_doc::register_doc! {
    name: "numel",
    aliases: [],
    section: "inspection",
    summary: "Number of Elements in an Array",
    body: r#"
## Usage
Returns the number of elements in an array `x`, or in a subindex
expression.  The syntax for its use is either

```text
   y = numel(x)
```
or

```text
   y = numel(x,varargin)
```
Generally, `numel` returns `prod(size(x))`, the number of total
elements in `x`.  However, you can specify a number of indexing
expressions for `varagin` such as `index1, index2, ..., indexm`.
In that case, the output of `numel` is
`prod(size(x(index1,...,indexm)))`.

## Example
For a `4 x 4 x 3` matrix, the length is `4`, not `48`, as you
might expect, but `numel` is `48`.

```fm-exec
x = rand(4,4,3);
length(x)
numel(x)
```

Here is an example of using `numel` with indexing expressions.

```fm-exec
numel(x,1:3,1:2,2)
```
"#,
}

fm_doc::register_doc! {
    name: "size",
    aliases: [],
    section: "inspection",
    summary: "Size of a Variable",
    body: r#"
## Usage
Returns the size of a variable.  There are two syntaxes for its
use.  The first syntax returns the size of the array as a vector
of integers, one integer for each dimension

```text
  [d1,d2,...,dn] = size(x)
```
The other format returns the size of `x` along a particular
dimension:

```text
  d = size(x,n)
```
where `n` is the dimension along which to return the size.

## Example

```fm-exec
a = randn(23,12,5);
size(a)
```

Here is an example of the second form of `size`.

```fm-exec
size(a,2)
```
"#,
}

fm_doc::register_doc! {
    name: "typeof",
    aliases: [],
    section: "inspection",
    summary: "Determine the Type of an Argument",
    body: r#"
## Usage
Returns a string describing the type of an array.  The syntax for its use is

```text
   y = typeof(x),
```
The returned string is one of
- `'cell'` for cell-arrays

- `'struct'` for structure-arrays

- `'logical'` for logical arrays

- `'uint8'` for unsigned 8-bit integers

- `'int8'` for signed 8-bit integers

- `'uint16'` for unsigned 16-bit integers

- `'int16'` for signed 16-bit integers

- `'uint32'` for unsigned 32-bit integers

- `'int32'` for signed 32-bit integers

- `'float'` for 32-bit floating point numbers

- `'double'` for 64-bit floating point numbers

- `'string'` for string arrays

## Example
The following piece of code demonstrates the output of the `typeof`
command for each possible type.  The first example is with a simple cell array.

```fm-exec
typeof({1})
```

The next example uses the `struct` constructor to make a simple scalar struct.

```fm-exec
typeof(struct('foo',3))
```

The next example uses a comparison between two scalar integers to generate
a scalar logical type.

```fm-exec
typeof(3>5)
```

For the integers, the typecast operations are used to generate the arguments.

```fm-exec
typeof(uint8(3))
typeof(int8(8))
typeof(uint16(3))
typeof(int16(8))
typeof(uint32(3))
typeof(int32(3))
typeof(uint64(3))
typeof(int64(3))
```

Float, and double can be created using the suffixes.

```fm-exec
typeof(1.0f)
typeof(1.0D)
typeof(1.0f+i)
typeof(1.0D+2.0D*i)
```
"#,
}

fm_doc::register_doc! {
    name: "what",
    aliases: [],
    section: "inspection",
    summary: "List FreeMat Files In Directory",
    body: r#"
## Usage
Lists files in a directory (or the current directory if no
argument is supplied) that are relevant to FreeMat.  These
are `M`-files, `MAT`-files, and class directories.  There
are several syntaxes for its use.  The first is

```text
   what
```
which lists the aforementioned items.  If you provide a
path instead

```text
   what path-to-folder
```
then `what` will list the relevant FreeMat items in the specified
directory.
"#,
}

fm_doc::register_doc! {
    name: "where",
    aliases: [],
    section: "inspection",
    summary: "Get Information on Program Stack",
    body: r#"
## Usage
Returns information on the current stack.  The usage is

```text
   where
```
The result is a kind of stack trace that indicates the state
of the current call stack, and where you are relative to the
stack.

## Example
Suppose we have the following chain of functions.

```fm-file:chain1.m
function chain1
  a = 32;
  b = a + 5;
  chain2(b)
```

```text
     chain1.m
```

```fm-file:chain2.m
function chain2(d)
  d = d + 5;
  chain3
```

```text
     chain2.m
```

```fm-file:chain3.m
function chain3
  g = 54;
  f = g + 1;
  keyboard
```

```text
     chain3.m
```

The execution of the `where` command shows the stack trace.

```fm-exec
chain1
where
```
"#,
}

fm_doc::register_doc! {
    name: "which",
    aliases: [],
    section: "inspection",
    summary: "Get Information on Function",
    body: r#"
## Usage
Returns information on a function (if defined).  The usage is

```text
   which(fname)
```
where `fname` is a `string` argument that contains the name of the
function.  For functions and scripts defined
via `.m` files, the `which` command returns the location of the source
file:

```text
   y = which(fname)
```
will return the filename for the `.m` file corresponding to the given
function, and an empty string otherwise.

## Example
First, we apply the `which` command to a built in function.

```fm-exec
which fft
```

Next, we apply it to a function defined via a `.m` file.

```fm-exec
which fliplr
```
"#,
}

fm_doc::register_doc! {
    name: "who",
    aliases: [],
    section: "inspection",
    summary: "Describe Currently Defined Variables",
    body: r#"
## Usage
Reports information on either all variables in the current context
or on a specified set of variables.  For each variable, the `who`
function indicates the size and type of the variable as well as
if it is a global or persistent.  There are two formats for the
function call.  The first is the explicit form, in which a list
of variables are provided:

```text
  who a1 a2 ...
```
In the second form

```text
  who
```
the `who` function lists all variables defined in the current
context (as well as global and persistent variables). Note that
there are two alternate forms for calling the `who` function:

```text
  who 'a1' 'a2' ...
```
and

```text
  who('a1','a2',...)
```

## Example
Here is an example of the general use of `who`, which lists all of the variables defined.

```fm-exec
c = [1,2,3];
f = 'hello';
p = randn(1,256);
who
```

In the second case, we examine only a specific variable:

```fm-exec
who c
who('c')
```
"#,
}

